This directory bundles plink.exe and pscp.exe from the PuTTY project
(https://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/putty/), used here for
unattended SSH password authentication - Windows' own built-in OpenSSH
client cannot read a password from a non-interactive stdin at all, so
there is no dependency-free way to script password-based SSH from
PowerShell without a different tool.

Install-ClientDebianSSH.ps1 writes the password to a short-lived,
current-user-only temp file and passes it via plink's/pscp's -pwfile
argument (added in PuTTY 0.81) rather than the -pw command-line argument,
so the password never appears in a local process listing (Get-CimInstance
Win32_Process, Task Manager) for the duration of the connection. The temp
file's contents are overwritten before deletion once the connection
completes.

plink.exe/pscp.exe are also used for SSH KEY authentication (not just
password), since 2026-09-15 - Windows' built-in ssh.exe/ssh-keyscan.exe
cannot negotiate the KEX algorithms recent OpenSSH servers offer (found
live against a real Debian 13 target), while the bundled PuTTY build
already handles them. The private key is converted from OpenSSH format
to PuTTY's own .ppk format entirely in-process by this project's own
PowerShell code (Convert-OpenSshKeyToPpk in Install-ClientDebianSSH.ps1/
Uninstall-ClientDebianSSH.ps1, RSA keys only) - PuTTY's own puttygen.exe
was investigated for this and rejected: the Windows build of puttygen.exe
is GUI-only and does not support any command-line conversion flags
(confirmed live, `-O`/`-o`/etc. all rejected as "unrecognised option");
Pageant (PuTTY's SSH agent) was also tried and also cannot load an
OpenSSH-format key directly ("Couldn't load this key (OpenSSH SSH-2
private key (new format))", confirmed live). No third PuTTY binary is
bundled here as a result - only plink.exe/pscp.exe, exactly as before.

